# Complexity Rules

Keep functions and components simple. Align with ESLint: **cyclomatic complexity ≤ 10** (see [eslint.config.mjs](../../eslint.config.mjs)).

## Cyclomatic complexity

Prefer early returns and small functions over many branches. Stay under the project limit of 10.

```typescript
// BAD: Many branches in one function
function getStatus(order: Order) {
  if (order.cancelled) return 'cancelled';
  if (order.shipped) return 'shipped';
  if (order.paid && !order.shipped) return 'processing';
  if (!order.paid) return 'pending';
  return 'unknown';
}

// GOOD: Extract or use a map/strategy; fewer branches per function
const statusRules: Array<{ check: (o: Order) => boolean; status: string }> = [
  { check: (o) => o.cancelled, status: 'cancelled' },
  { check: (o) => o.shipped, status: 'shipped' },
  // ...
];
function getStatus(order: Order) {
  return statusRules.find((r) => r.check(order))?.status ?? 'unknown';
}
```

## Function size and nesting

Keep functions short and focused (e.g. under ~20–30 lines). Avoid nesting deeper than about 3 levels; use early returns and guard clauses.

```typescript
// BAD: Deep nesting, long function
function processUser(input: unknown) {
  if (input) {
    const data = validate(input);
    if (data) {
      const user = mapToUser(data);
      if (user.active) {
        await save(user);
        await notify(user);
      }
    }
  }
}

// GOOD: Early returns, extracted helpers
function processUser(input: unknown) {
  const data = validate(input);
  if (!data) return;
  const user = mapToUser(data);
  if (!user.active) return;
  await save(user);
  await notify(user);
}
```

## Cognitive simplicity

One clear purpose per function. Avoid clever or dense logic; favor readable, linear flow.

When suggesting or refactoring code, keep complexity low: respect the complexity limit, small functions, shallow nesting, and early returns.
